ARTICLE III — OFFER TO ADDRESSEES (SCOPE DEFINED BY ASPIRATION)

3.1 The Author extends the permissions described in Section 2.1 to an
    Addressee if, and only for so long as, the Addressee holds the
    Aspiration (the "Aspiration Condition"). The Aspiration Condition
    defines the scope of the offer made to Addressees: an Addressee who
    does not hold the Aspiration is not offered, and does not receive,
    any license under this Article, and as to such person all rights in
    the Software are reserved.

3.2 Self-Certification. The Aspiration is an internal state. An
    Addressee's exercise of any permission under Section 3.1
    constitutes the Addressee's representation that the Aspiration
    Condition is satisfied. That representation is conclusive: no
    person may demand proof of it, conduct inquiry into it, or contest
    it, and no evidence shall be received against it.

3.3 Deemed Satisfaction. An Addressee whose self-expression already
    meets or exceeds the standard described in Section 1.5 is
    conclusively deemed to satisfy the Aspiration Condition.

3.4 Lapse and Revival. If an Addressee ceases to hold the Aspiration,
    the license under Section 3.1 terminates automatically and
    prospectively; it revives automatically, without formality, upon
    the Aspiration being held anew. Acts performed while the license
    was in force remain licensed.

3.5 No Compulsion. Nothing in this License requires any person to hold
    the Aspiration. The sole consequence of not holding it is the
    absence of a license under this Article.

